'''''Senecio sylvaticus''''' is a species of flowering plant in the [[asteraceae|aster family]]. It is variously known as the '''woodland ragwort''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=SESY|taxon=Senecio sylvaticus|accessdate=10 November 2015}}</ref> '''heath groundsel''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 }}</ref> or '''mountain [[Senecio vulgaris|common groundsel]]'''.<ref>{{Cite EB9|wstitle=Groundsel |volume=11 |page=221}}</ref> It is native to [[Eurasia]], and it can be found in other places, including western and eastern sections of North America, as an [[introduced species]] and an occasional roadside weed. It grows best in cool, wet areas. It is an annual herb producing a single erect stem up to 80 centimeters tall from a [[taproot]]. It is coated in short, curly hairs. The toothed, deeply lobed leaves are up to 12 centimeters long and borne on [[Petiole (botany)|petioles]]. They are evenly distributed along the stem. The [[inflorescence]] is a wide, spreading array of many [[Head (botany)|flower heads]], each lined with green- or black-tipped [[bract|phyllaries]]. The heads contain yellow disc florets and most have very tiny yellow ray florets as well.

